When viscous fluid of mass $m$ is dropped and start spreading out then its moment of inertia increases and angular velocity decreases. But when it start falling then its moment of inertia again starts decreasing and angular velocity increases.

When solid sphere rolls down on an inclined plane, then it has both rotational and translational kinetic energy
or $\quad \begin{array}{ll} & K=K_{\text {rot }}+K_{\text {trans }} \\ & K=\frac{1}{2} l \omega^2+\frac{1}{2} M v^2\end{array}
where, $I=$ moment of inertia of solid sphere $=\frac{2}{5} M R^2\begin{aligned} \therefore \quad K & =\frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{2}{5} M(2 R)^2\right) \omega^2+\frac{1}{2} M v^2 & {[\because R=2 R] } \\ & =\frac{4}{5} M R^2\left(\frac{v}{2 R}\right)^2+\frac{7}{2} M v^2 & {[\because v=r \omega] }\end{aligned}=\frac{1}{5} M v^2+\frac{1}{2} M v^2=\frac{1}{10} M v^2$
Now, gain in $\mathrm{KE}=$ loss in PE
$\begin{aligned} \frac{7}{10} M v^2 & =M g h \\ v & =\sqrt{\frac{10}{7} g h}\end{aligned}
